If you're looking for point-wise explanations of various grammar applications (i.e., how grammar rules are applied in speech and writing), here's a list of key points:\par
\par
1. Sentence Structure\par
Simple Sentences: A sentence that contains only one independent clause (e.g., "She reads every day.").\par
Compound Sentences: A sentence made by joining two independent clauses with a coordinating conjunction (e.g., "I want to go to the beach, but it is raining.").\par
Complex Sentences: A sentence containing one independent clause and at least one dependent clause (e.g., "Although it was raining, she went for a walk.").\par
Compound-Complex Sentences: A sentence that has two independent clauses and at least one dependent clause (e.g., "I wanted to go to the park, but it started raining after I left home.").\par
2. Parts of Speech\par
Nouns: Used to name people, places, things, or ideas (e.g., "dog," "city," "love").\par
Pronouns: Words that take the place of nouns (e.g., "he," "she," "it").\par
Verbs: Action or state of being words (e.g., "run," "is," "play").\par
Adjectives: Words that describe or modify nouns (e.g., "beautiful," "quick," "happy").\par
Adverbs: Words that describe or modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs (e.g., "quickly," "very," "extremely").\par
Prepositions: Words that show relationships between nouns (e.g., "in," "on," "at").\par
Conjunctions: Words that connect words, phrases, or clauses (e.g., "and," "but," "because").\par
Interjections: Words or phrases that express strong emotions (e.g., "wow," "ouch," "hey").\par
3. Tenses\par
Present Simple: Used to express habits, facts, or general truths (e.g., "She studies every day.").\par
Past Simple: Used to express actions completed in the past (e.g., "He went to the store yesterday.").\par
Future Simple: Used to express actions that will happen (e.g., "I will call you later.").\par
Present Continuous: Used for actions happening right now (e.g., "She is reading a book.").\par
Past Continuous: Used for actions happening at a specific time in the past (e.g., "They were playing when I arrived.").\par
Future Continuous: Used for actions that will be happening at a specific time in the future (e.g., "I will be studying at 8 PM.").\par
Present Perfect: Used to describe actions that occurred at an unspecified time or have relevance to the present (e.g., "I have finished my homework.").\par
Past Perfect: Describes an action that was completed before another action in the past (e.g., "She had left before I arrived.").\par
Future Perfect: Describes an action that will be completed before a specific time in the future (e.g., "By next week, I will have finished the project.").\par
4. Subject-Verb Agreement\par
The subject and verb must agree in number (singular or plural). For example:\par
Singular subject: "The cat runs fast."\par
Plural subject: "The cats run fast."\par
Irregular plural nouns: "The children play outside," not "The children plays outside."\par
In sentences with collective nouns, the verb can be singular or plural depending on whether the noun refers to a single unit or multiple members (e.g., "The team is winning" vs. "The team are arguing among themselves.").\par
5. Modifiers\par
Adjective Placement: Adjectives usually go before the noun they modify (e.g., "a red car").\par
Adverb Placement: Adverbs typically modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs, and their placement can vary. For example:\par
"She sings beautifully." (adverb modifying verb)\par
"She is incredibly talented." (adverb modifying adjective)\par
"She sings very beautifully." (adverb modifying another adverb).\par
6. Articles\par
Definite Article ("the") is used to refer to something specific or known (e.g., "I saw the movie you recommended.").\par
Indefinite Articles ("a" and "an") are used for something general or when it's mentioned for the first time:\par
"I want a sandwich." (any sandwich)\par
"I saw an owl in the tree." (any owl, not a specific one).\par
7. Pronoun Usage\par
Personal Pronouns: Represent specific people or things (e.g., "he," "she," "it," "they").\par
Possessive Pronouns: Indicate ownership (e.g., "mine," "yours," "his," "hers").\par
Reflexive Pronouns: Used when the subject and object of the sentence are the same (e.g., "I taught myself to play the piano.").\par
Relative Pronouns: Link clauses or phrases to nouns (e.g., "who," "whom," "whose," "which," "that").\par
8. Conditionals\par
Zero Conditional: Used to talk about facts or general truths (e.g., "If you heat water to 100\'b0C, it boils.").\par
First Conditional: Used to talk about real or possible situations in the future (e.g., "If it rains, I will take an umbrella.").\par
Second Conditional: Used for hypothetical situations in the present or future (e.g., "If I had a million dollars, I would travel the world.").\par
Third Conditional: Used for hypothetical situations in the past (e.g., "If I had studied harder, I would have passed the exam.").\par
9. Punctuation\par
Commas: Used to separate items in a list, after introductory words, or to set off nonessential information (e.g., "I bought apples, oranges, and bananas." or "After the meeting, we went for lunch.").\par
Period (Full Stop): Marks the end of a declarative sentence (e.g., "She is a teacher.").\par
Question Marks: Used at the end of a direct question (e.g., "How are you?").\par
Exclamation Marks: Used to express strong feelings or emotions (e.g., "What a beautiful sunset!").\par
Apostrophes: Indicate possession (e.g., "Sarah's book") or form contractions (e.g., "can't" for "cannot").\par
Quotation Marks: Used to enclose direct speech, quotations, or titles (e.g., "He said, 'I'll be there soon.'").\par
10. Prepositions\par
Time Prepositions: "At" (for specific times), "on" (for days and dates), "in" (for months, years, seasons, etc.) (e.g., "She will arrive at 5 PM," "He was born on Monday," "They visit us in winter.").\par
Place Prepositions: "At" (for specific places), "in" (for enclosed spaces), "on" (for surfaces) (e.g., "I am at the door," "She is in the room," "The book is on the table.").\par
These grammar applications form the foundation for constructing clear, correct, and effective sentences. By understanding and applying these rules, you can improve both written and spoken communication.\par
