Grammar Application: Tense (With Examples)\par
Tenses in grammar are used to indicate when an action or event takes place \f1\emdash  in the present, past, or future. Understanding how to apply different tenses allows you to communicate more clearly and precisely. Below are the main tenses, with examples for each:\par
\par
1. Present Tense\par
The present tense is used to describe actions or situations that are happening right now, habitual actions, or general truths.\par
\par
Present Simple Tense\par
Usage: To express habits, routines, and general truths.\par
Example: "She reads books every day." (habit)\par
Example: "The Earth revolves around the Sun." (general truth)\par
Present Continuous Tense\par
Usage: To describe actions happening right now or in the near future.\par
Example: "I am reading a book right now."\par
Example: "They are playing soccer in the park."\par
Present Perfect Tense\par
Usage: To describe actions that occurred at an unspecified time and have relevance to the present.\par
Example: "I have finished my homework." (The homework is done, and it matters now.)\par
Present Perfect Continuous Tense\par
Usage: To describe actions that started in the past and are still continuing, or recently completed actions with emphasis on duration.\par
Example: "She has been working on the project for hours."\par
Example: "I have been waiting for you for 30 minutes."\par
2. Past Tense\par
The past tense describes actions or events that have already happened.\par
\par
Past Simple Tense\par
Usage: To describe actions completed in the past at a specific time.\par
Example: "I visited the museum yesterday."\par
Example: "He studied hard for the exam."\par
Past Continuous Tense\par
Usage: To describe actions that were happening at a specific time in the past.\par
Example: "I was reading when you called me."\par
Example: "They were watching TV at 8 PM last night."\par
Past Perfect Tense\par
Usage: To describe an action that was completed before another action in the past.\par
Example: "She had finished the book before I arrived."\par
Example: "By the time I woke up, he had already left."\par
Past Perfect Continuous Tense\par
Usage: To describe an action that started in the past, continued for a period, and ended before another action in the past.\par
Example: "I had been studying for two hours when the power went out."\par
Example: "They had been waiting for an hour before the train arrived."\par
3. Future Tense\par
The future tense describes actions or events that will happen after the present moment.\par
\par
Future Simple Tense\par
Usage: To describe actions that will happen in the future.\par
Example: "I will call you later."\par
Example: "They will travel to Japan next year."\par
Future Continuous Tense\par
Usage: To describe actions that will be happening at a specific time in the future.\par
Example: "I will be studying at 9 PM tonight."\par
Example: "At this time tomorrow, we will be flying to New York."\par
Future Perfect Tense\par
Usage: To describe actions that will be completed before a specific time in the future.\par
Example: "By next week, I will have finished the project."\par
Example: "They will have left by the time we arrive."\par
Future Perfect Continuous Tense\par
Usage: To describe actions that will continue until a specific time in the future and emphasize duration.\par
Example: "By 2025, she will have been working here for 10 years."\par
Example: "In two weeks, I will have been living in this city for a year."\par
